To output a PDF in SAS with a journal format, you can use the `ods pdf` statement, which allows you to create and customize PDF files in SAS. Here's an example code snippet:
```sas
ods pdf file="journal_format.pdf" startpage=no;
title "Journal Article";
footnote "This is a sample journal article";

ods region;
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
  format make $20. model $30. trim 8. price 7.3;
  var make model trim price;
  run;
ods region;
ods endr;
ods pdf close;
```
Let's break down the code:

1. `ods pdf file="journal_format.pdf" startpage=no;` specifies the output file name, `journal_format.pdf`, and sets the `startpage` option to `no`, which means the first page will not be titled with the default SAS title.
2. `title "Journal Article";` sets the title of the journal article.
3. `footnote "This is a sample journal article";` adds a footnote to the page.
4. `ods region;` begins a new region, which will contain the output of your PROC PRINT statement.
5. `proc print data=sashelp.cars; ... run;` prints the `CARS` dataset in the usual way.
6. `ods region;` ends the region.
7. `ods endr;` ends the output destination.
8. `ods pdf close;` closes the PDF file.

The resulting PDF file will have a journal-style format, with a title, footnote, and the PROC PRINT output. You can customize the layout and appearance of the PDF using various SAS options and styles.

Some additional tips:

